    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/abstract": [
      {
        "@value": "Successful transition to data-driven personalized medicine requires dedicated programs and nationally representative data resources to fine-map local populations genetic structure and ensure equitable access to benefits of emerging genomic medicine. This is particularly critical in Switzerland whose contemporary population is among the most genetically diverse in Europe. The Genome of Switzerland (GOS) project aims to create the first Swiss national reference genomic dataset characterizing the Swiss population genetic structure. The ETH PHRT funded GOS Pilot Phase provides proof-of-principle through systematic mapping of eligible samples across Switzerland and clinical-grade sequencing of 1,000 whole-genomes capturing the diversity characteristic to Swiss urban areas.  Although conceived as a feasibility project, the GOS-Pilot has delivered initial scientific, operational, and societal insights. Participant trust and willingness to engage exceeded expectations: among individuals randomly selected from the CHUV Genomic Biobank for the GOS-Pilot, only 2.5% declined participation. Preliminary analyses confirm marked heterogeneity in the Swiss population genetic architecture, underlining the need for comprehensive national reference data and interoperability with Pan-European resources. Immediate utility of the GOS-Pilot was demonstrated through gene-wise allele look-ups and exemplified by the observation of a high prevalence of pharmacogenomic variants (mean: 3.9 per individual) and variability in drug response linked to common pharmacogenes. The GOS-Pilot provides the foundation for future national scale-up and alignment with the Genome of Europe initiative to enable precise diagnostics and equitable access to genomic medicine in Switzerland. The GOS data will be accessible to authorized researchers and clinicians through the Swiss fEGA repository."
      }
    ],
